  "description": "The proof, the commitment, the SAS, the hub ID display and the sidecar window. \"ekm\" gives the exporter's parameters: its label, a context of the 16 raw pid bytes, and its length; the EKMs below are synthetic, since only a live TLS connection gives a real one. A proof case's \"messageHex\" is the ASCII of draftyak/pair/v1 followed by \"ekmHex\", \"pubsHex\" and, in a claim's case, the 32 bytes of the commitment \"cHex\"; \"valid\" says whether the presented \"proofHex\" equals HMAC-SHA-256 keyed with \"secHex\" over that message (lengths first, then in constant time). A commitment case's \"c\" is the SHA-256 of its \"messageHex\", the ASCII of draftyak/sas-commit/v1 followed by \"ndHex\". A SAS case's \"sha256\" is the SHA-256 of its \"messageHex\", the ASCII of draftyak/sas/v1 followed by \"ekmHex\", \"ndHex\" and \"nhHex\", and \"sas\" is the big-endian u32 of its first 4 bytes mod 1,000,000, as six digits with leading zeros. A hub ID's \"display\" is DYK- and the first 16 hex digits of \"hid\" in upper case, in groups of four. A sidecar case's \"pid\" and \"sec\" are base64url of 16 bytes of HKDF-SHA-256 over \"bootstrapSecretHex\" with an empty salt and the info draftyak/pair/v1/sidecar-pid or draftyak/pair/v1/sidecar-sec. Ids starting with skeleton are the S10 transport skeleton's own numbers (ADR 0012's kept test).",
